## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical problem solving for shared Linux runtimes. It focuses on beginner-friendly CPU isolation using cgroup v2 primitives rather than app changes.

## Key Concepts
- cgroup v2 CPU controller (cpu.max, cpu.weight)
- systemd slices or cpuset for per-tenant isolation
- basic benchmarking of latency under bursts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Reads current cpu.max for a given cgroup path
const fs = require('fs');
try {
  const quota = fs.readFileSync('/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.slice/cpu.max','utf8').trim();
  console.log('cpu.max:', quota);
} catch (e) { console.error(e); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this plan to handle bursty tenants with varying baselines?
- What risks exist with aggressive quotas and how would you detect them?

## Why This Is Asked
Handles a realistic, repeatable issue in multi-tenant Linux environments. Evaluates practical debugging, safe isolation, and rollback discipline.

## Key Concepts
- inotify and max_user_watches
- per-tenant resource control (ulimit, cgroups)
- observability and test planning
- safe rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple Node.js watcher demo illustrating per-tenant file activity
const fs = require('fs');
const path = '/ci-scratch/tenant-A';
fs.watch(path, {recursive: true}, (ev, f) => {
  console.log(`[watch] ${ev}: ${f}`);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate per-tenant limit adjustments and verification?
- How would you validate rollback in a production window without impacting running builds?
